<article class="env-proposition" id="012R">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/012R">Proposition <span data-tag="012R">1.1.3.6</span>.</a> Let $S^{-}$ and $S^{+}$ be simplicial sets having dimensions $\leq k_{-}$ and $\leq k_{+}$, respectively. Then the product $S^{-} \times S^{+}$ has dimension $\leq k_{-} + k_{+}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\sigma = (\sigma _{-}, \sigma _{+})$ be a nondegenerate $n$-simplex of the product $S^{-} \times S^{+}$. Using Proposition <a data-tag="0014" href="/tag/0014">1.1.3.8</a>, we see that $\sigma _{-}$ and $\sigma _{+}$ admit factorizations 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \Delta ^{n} \xrightarrow { \alpha _{-} } \Delta ^{n_{-}} \xrightarrow { \tau _{-} } S^{-} \quad \quad \Delta ^{n} \xrightarrow { \alpha _{+} } \Delta ^{n_{+}} \xrightarrow { \tau _{+} } S^{+}, \]
</div>
<p>
       where $\tau _{-}$ and $\tau _{+}$ are nondegenerate, so that $n_{-} \leq k_{-}$ and $n_{+} \leq k_{+}$. It follows that $\sigma $ factors as a composition 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \Delta ^{n} \xrightarrow { (\alpha _{-}, \alpha _{+} ) } \Delta ^{n_{-}} \times \Delta ^{n_{+}} \xrightarrow { \tau _{-} \times \tau _{+} } S^{-} \times S^{+}. \]
</div>
<p>
       The nondegeneracy of $\sigma $ guarantees that the map of partially ordered sets $[n] \xrightarrow { (\alpha _{-}, \alpha _{+} )} [n_{-} ] \times [n_{+} ]$ is a monomorphism, so that $n \leq n_{-} + n_{+} \leq k_{-} + k_{+}$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></p></p></article>
